"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][20]={"university_biology_en:M20-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M20-L1 – Signalling networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Advanced Cell and Molecular Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>: signalling, kinetics\nand differential equations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse signalling networks as\ndynamic systems, calculate steady states and distinguish temporal signal\npatterns from mean activity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A receptor translates an extracellular cue into intracellular\nactivity. Its effect depends not only on messenger concentration but\nalso on signal strength, duration and temporal organisation. This\nmaster’s elective builds on cell biology and biochemistry by treating\ndynamics as a measurable system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"network-architecture-and-dynamics\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Network architecture and\ndynamics<\/h3>\n<p>In a kinase cascade successive stages activate additional components.\nPhosphatases can reverse phosphorylation. Scaffold proteins and\ncompartments influence which reactions are spatially possible. Branches\ngenerate distinct outputs, while convergence integrates multiple inputs.\nPositive feedback can stabilise switch-like behaviour under suitable\nconditions; negative feedback can limit amplification or favour\nadaptation and, with sufficient delay, oscillation. Topology alone does\nnot prove a particular dynamic regime: parameters, initial conditions\nand timescales also matter.<\/p>\n<p>A two-state model for the active fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween zero and one is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dx}{dt}=a(1-x)-bx,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare effective activation and deactivation rates in inverse time units.\nThe steady-state fraction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=a/(a+b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand relaxation time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/(a+b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt constant input,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x^*+(x(0)-x^*)e^{-t/\\tau}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis model conserves total abundance and contains no explicit\nfeedback.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"from-signal-trajectories-to-evidence\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From signal trajectories\nto evidence<\/h3>\n<p>A time average may conceal pulses. In an experimental study Albeck\nand colleagues examined time-varying ERK activity and its relationship\nto S-phase entry. An individual fluorescence signal may nevertheless\ncombine reporter kinetics with biological activity. Model assessment\ncompares time trajectories, independent units, alternative explanations\nand uncertainty in estimated parameters. Correlation between a pulse\nfeature and cell fate alone does not establish a necessary or sufficient\ncause.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_105_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Relaxation towards a steady state and two signals with equal time means but different threshold crossings.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Relaxation towards a steady state and two\nsignals with equal time means but different threshold\ncrossings.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Relaxation towards a steady state and two signals with\nequal time means but different threshold crossings.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Simulated rates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.6\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.4\\,\\mathrm{min}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStarting from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nafter two minutes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.519<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(2)=0.6(1-e^{-2})\\approx0.519<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo idealised signals over ten minutes have the same integral of 5\nactivity-minutes: A remains at 0.5; B stays at one for the first five\nminutes and at zero for the next five. A downstream nonlinear threshold\nof 0.8 would be crossed only by B. The time mean alone is insufficient\nto predict the response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the steady-state fraction and relaxation time for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper minute.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot a single steady-state measurement uniquely determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which measurement could distinguish a pulse model from a constant\nsignal?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=2\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The steady-state fraction determines only the rate ratio. A time\nseries with known stimulation can additionally constrain the sum of\nrates.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Adequately time-resolved single-cell trajectories using a validated\nreporter and multiple independent biological units.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The model ignores synthesis, degradation, spatial distribution and\nstochastic fluctuations. All numbers are illustrative. The threshold\nresponse is hypothetical, not a universal ERK rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Dynamic networks explain why signal shape and strength must be\nconsidered separately. The next lesson examines regulatory layers at the\ngenome.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23219535/\">Albeck, Mills\n&amp; Brugge (2013) – Frequency-modulated pulses of ERK activity\ntransmit quantitative proliferation signals<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Abstract: temporal ERK\nactivity and cell-to-cell variability; indexed abstract · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M20-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M20-L2 – Chromatin and genome regulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Advanced Cell and Molecular Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L2<\/a>: chromatin,\nepigenetics and functional genetics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Integrate regulatory genomic\ndata, distinguish candidates from causally supported elements and\ninterpret a simple enrichment analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A genome contains regulatory regions as well as protein-coding\nsegments. Which regions participate functionally in a cell type cannot\nbe inferred from sequence alone. Chromatin state, transcription factors\nand spatial organisation influence the accessibility and use of\nregulatory DNA.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"measurement-layers-and-regulatory-hypotheses\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement layers\nand regulatory hypotheses<\/h3>\n<p>ATAC-seq provides chromatin accessibility data; ChIP-seq measures\nenrichment of particular bound proteins or histone modifications.\nRNA-seq supplies an expression profile, while contact-based data can\ndescribe spatial proximity in the genome. These methods produce\ndifferent measurements, not interchangeable proofs. H3K27ac, for\nexample, is commonly used as an indicator of active regulatory regions\nbut does not establish a direct effect on a particular target gene. An\nenhancer can be distant from a promoter; the nearest gene need not be\nthe true target.<\/p>\n<p>ENCODE catalogues candidate regulatory elements using genome-wide\ndata. Comparing datasets requires attention to reference genome, cell\ntype, assay, quality indicators and replicates. Interval overlap is\nmeaningful only after harmonising coordinate systems. A hypothesis gains\nsupport when independent evidence layers agree. Causal evidence\nsupported by experiments or appropriate natural variation remains\ndistinct.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"enrichment-and-background\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Enrichment and background<\/h3>\n<p>An overlap analysis requires a biologically and technically\ncomparable universe. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsurveyed regions, of which\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare annotated, and a selection of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nregions, random sampling without replacement gives an expected hit count\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=nK/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ratio of observed to expected hits describes enrichment. The\nhypergeometric distribution is a possible null model if regions can\ngenuinely be treated as exchangeable. Genomic clustering, length\ndifferences and accessibility bias can violate this assumption.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_106_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of annotated fractions in background and selection; expected and observed hits.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of annotated fractions in\nbackground and selection; expected and observed hits.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Comparison of annotated fractions in background and\nselection; expected and observed hits.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional dataset contains 1000 comparable regions, 200 with a\nregulatory annotation. Among 50 selected regions, 20 are annotated.\nExpected hits are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\cdot200/1000=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nenrichment is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20/10=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe 40% in the selected set is twice the 20% in the background. This is\nneither an error probability nor the fraction of causally functional\nelements. A second evidence layer could prioritise candidates without\nautomatically confirming them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate expectation and enrichment for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=800<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nobserved\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does overlap with a chromatin mark not prove function?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two properties by which comparable background regions should be\nselected.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nenrichment\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15/5=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The mark is an associated signal; effect, target gene and direction\nare not uniquely identified.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example interval length, cell type, accessibility, GC content or\ntechnical detectability.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The numbers are simulated. Statistical significance requires a\nsuitable null distribution, accounting for multiple tests and\nindependent validation. Epigenetic marks are context-dependent and not\nautomatically heritable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Regulatory annotations generate testable hypotheses. The next lesson\naddresses quantitative measurement at the image level.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.genome.gov/Funded-Programs-Projects/ENCODE-Project-ENCyclopedia-Of-DNA-Elements\">NHGRI\n– The Encyclopedia of DNA Elements (ENCODE)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Project overview:\ncandidate regulatory elements and human/mouse datasets; indexed page ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M20-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M20-L3 – Quantitative image analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Advanced Cell and Molecular Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>: microscopy and\nindependent experimental units.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess segmentation quality,\nbackground-corrected intensity and measurement uncertainty\nquantitatively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A microscopy image is a discrete measurement, not an unchanged\nobject. Optical blur, detector noise, background and limited dynamic\nrange affect the image. Quantitative image analysis translates pixels\ninto biologically interpretable features and must explicitly consider\nerrors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"measurement-chain-and-features\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement chain and\nfeatures<\/h3>\n<p>Preserve raw data, calibration and metadata. Segmentation defines a\nmask for cells, nuclei or other structures. Area, shape, integrated\nintensity or mean intensity can be derived from the mask. With pixel\nside length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npixels, area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=ns^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor pixel intensities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an estimated constant background\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\sum_i I_i-nB<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMean corrected intensity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrated and mean intensities address different questions; cell size\nand marker abundance may correlate. Subtraction cannot repair\nsaturation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"validation-and-statistical-unit\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validation and statistical\nunit<\/h3>\n<p>Compare an automated mask\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith an independently reviewed reference mask\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Dice coefficient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=2|P\\cap G|/(|P|+|G|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt lies between zero and one when the masks are not both empty. A high\nscore may nevertheless fail to capture small biologically important\nstructures. Assess counting errors, oversegmentation and merged objects\nseparately. A test set should be independent of method selection or\ntuning and cover different image qualities.<\/p>\n<p>Multiple cells per image and images per sample create nested\nmeasurements. For group comparisons, the level of independent treatment\nassignment matters. Large pixel or cell counts cannot replace\nindependent samples. Report image processing, exclusion criteria,\ncalibration, versions and the relationship between raw data and result\nfigures. Sensitivity analyses using plausible alternative masks can\nreveal whether a conclusion depends on arbitrary segmentation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_107_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic mask overlap with pixel counts and Dice coefficient; not area-proportional.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic mask overlap with pixel counts\nand Dice coefficient; not area-proportional.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic mask overlap with pixel counts and Dice\ncoefficient; not area-proportional.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A simulated cell mask contains 120 pixels at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0.2\\,\\mu\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe intensity sum is 3600 detector units and background is 10 per pixel.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=4.8\\,\\mu\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=2400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mean corrected intensity is 20. A reference mask contains 100\npixels, with 90 in the intersection. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>220<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.818<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=180/220\\approx0.818<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDespite substantial overlap, area differs by 20% relative to the\nreference. Segmentation quality must therefore be evaluated in relation\nto the outcome.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A mask contains 80 pixels at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0.5\\,\\mu\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nintensity sum 2400 and background 5. Calculate area and corrected mean\nintensity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For two masks of 100 pixels each with 75 shared pixels, calculate\nDice.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should 200 cells from two independently treated samples not\nautomatically be treated as 200 independent replicates?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=80\\cdot0.5^2=20\\,\\mu\\mathrm m^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2400<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2400-80\\cdot5)/80=25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>200<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=150/200=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Cells within a sample share treatment and context. The independent\nunit here is the sample, not each cell; the hierarchy must be\nmodelled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All image values are simulated. Dice assumes comparable coordinates\nand an appropriate reference. Intensities are not absolute molecular\ncounts without additional calibration. The overview does not replace\nsupervised instrument training.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Quantitative imaging combines measurement and validation. The next\nlesson transfers these principles to single-cell datasets.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41592-023-01987-9\">Community-developed\nchecklists for publishing images and image analyses (2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nReporting acquisition, processing and analysis; indexed article · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/jmi.13208\">Made to measure: An\nintroduction to quantifying microscopy data in the life sciences<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nQuantification goal, segmentation, measurement limitations; indexed\narticle · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M20-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M20-L4 – Single-cell and omics methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Advanced Cell and Molecular Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>: omics, analysis and\ncell states.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish single-cell data\nstructures, quality control, cell-state annotation and donor-level\ninference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Bulk RNA-seq averages over a mixture of cells. Single-cell RNA-seq\nenables the study of heterogeneous cell states but does not capture\nevery RNA molecule. The measurement matrix often contains many zeros for\nboth biological and technical reasons. The central challenge is to\nseparate signal, technical variation and sampling structure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"data-matrix-and-analysis-levels\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data matrix and analysis\nlevels<\/h3>\n<p>A count matrix contains recorded counts for each gene and cell. Cell\nand gene metadata include information such as sample, donor, batch and\nannotation. Before comparison, assess quality indicators, unusual\nmulticell profiles and potential background signal. Thresholds depend on\ntissue and assay; rigid filtering may discard real rare states.\nNormalisation accounts for differences in captured library size but\ncannot automatically distinguish global biological changes from\ntechnical effects.<\/p>\n<p>Dimensionality reduction (such as PCA) compresses variation.\nNonlinear embeddings often serve visualisation; distances and apparent\nclusters do not directly establish lineage. Marker profiles, reference\ndata and domain expertise support annotations. Pseudotime orders states\nalong an assumed trajectory; it is not directly measured time and does\nnot establish lineage without additional evidence.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"biological-level-inference\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Biological-level inference<\/h3>\n<p>For condition-related differential expression, counts within a cell\npopulation may be aggregated per independent biological sample\n(pseudobulk) and analysed with an appropriate count model. This avoids\ntreating thousands of cells from the same donor as independent donors.\nSample size, design, batch confounding and cell-type composition remain\ncrucial. Proteomics, single-cell chromatin accessibility and spatial\ntranscriptomics measure different features; agreement supplies\ncomplementary evidence, not necessarily identical results.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_108_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three independent donors per group; cells are aggregated per donor, not counted as independent donors.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three independent donors per group; cells\nare aggregated per donor, not counted as independent\ndonors.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three independent donors per group; cells are aggregated\nper donor, not counted as independent donors.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional data: Two groups have three independent donors each. For\none cell type, 100 cells are captured per donor, giving 600 cells.\nAggregated counts for gene G per donor are 100, 120, 80 in A and 200,\n240, 160 in B. Under the simplified assumption of equal library sizes,\nthe mean ratio is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200/100=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(2)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThere are six independent donors, not 600; the ratio alone does not\nestablish significance. Real analyses incorporate library sizes,\nvariance models and appropriate multiple-testing correction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Three donors per group yield 250 cells each. How many cells and\nindependent donors are there?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate log2 fold change for mean values of 80 and 40 (B/A).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot complete confounding of batch and condition simply be\nresolved through integration?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">1500 cells and six independent donors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>80<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(40/80)=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both effects are unidentifiable in the design; computational\ncorrection requires additional assumptions or independent\ninformation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The counts are illustrative, not real. Pseudobulk is one possible\napproach rather than a universal method; paired or repeated samples\nrequire explicit accounting for dependence. Personal genomic data may be\nsubject to special access and privacy requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Single-cell analysis requires biological replication and critical\ninterpretation. The final lesson integrates assessment of different\nevidence types.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.15252/msb.20188746\">Luecken &amp; Theis\n(2019) – Current best practices in single-cell RNA-seq analysis: a\ntutorial<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Quality control, normalization, annotation and inference\npitfalls; indexed article · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M20-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M20-L5 – Critical reading of research literature<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Advanced Cell and Molecular Biology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a> to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L4<\/a>: research questions,\ninference and assays.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Critically evaluate a molecular\nbiology publication through its hypotheses, evidence chains, replication\nand alternative explanations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>An impressive figure or a small p-value alone does not establish a\nrobust mechanistic conclusion. Critical reading separates observed\neffects, statistical inference and the proposed biological mechanism.\nThe aim is a reasoned assessment rather than blanket acceptance or\nrejection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"evidence-chain-and-design\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence chain and design<\/h3>\n<p>First record the precise claim and the data intended to support it.\nDetermine whether the design can investigate association, temporal order\nor causal effects. Controls must address alternative explanations.\nIndependent biological units, random assignment, blinded assessment and\npredefined exclusions may reduce bias. Their necessity and feasibility\ndepend on design. Distinguish technical repeats from biological\nreplication.<\/p>\n<p>For figures, examine units, axes, sample size, variability, links to\nraw data, image processing and selection of representative examples. For\nomics, assess normalisation, model choice, batch, multiple tests and\nindependent validation. An effect in a model organism or cell line does\nnot automatically generalise to other tissues or humans. An orthogonal\nmeasurement may have different sources of error; shared systematic\nerrors remain possible.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"quantitative-assessment-and-synthesis\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quantitative assessment\nand synthesis<\/h3>\n<p>Extract effect size and uncertainty. A confidence interval including\nzero does not prove equality. A statistically supported small effect\nneed not be biologically meaningful. Check whether the research question\nwas predefined, whether findings are exploratory and whether all\nrelevant outcomes are reported. For conflicting studies, compare models,\npopulations, timescales and measurement definitions rather than just\ncounts of significant results. Reproducible analysis and independent\nreplication are distinct criteria.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_109_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two fictional effect sizes with confidence intervals; only the second excludes zero.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two fictional effect sizes with\nconfidence intervals; only the second excludes zero.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two fictional effect sizes with confidence intervals;\nonly the second excludes zero.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional publication measures a reporter in three independent\nsamples per group. Group means are 1.0 and 1.3 relative units. The\nestimated difference is 0.3, with a 95% confidence interval from -0.1 to\n0.7. The relative difference is 30%, but the interval is compatible with\nzero and positive effects. The claim of unequivocal pathway activation\nis not adequately supported. An independent study with a second\nmeasurement could assess the hypothesis; the reporter’s effect on\nbiological activity must also be considered. The interval is supplied\nfor illustration, not calculated from means alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A second experiment yields a difference of 0.05 with interval 0.01\nto 0.09. What does this not establish?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is repeating the analysis by two people not independent\nbiological replication?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Write a cautious results statement for the first experiment.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The interval lies above zero, but biological relevance, mechanism\nand generalisability do not automatically follow.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both use the same biological data; the repeat may assess analysis\nreproducibility only.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The estimated reporter difference is positive but considerably\nuncertain; no effect is compatible with the reported interval.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All values are fictional. Under model assumptions a 95% interval has\n95% long-run coverage, not a 95% probability for a fixed unknown\nparameter to lie within this particular interval. A report may omit\nimportant details without that alone establishing misconduct.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This master’s elective ends with an evidence audit. Further elective\nprofiles can be selected according to interests and prerequisites; they\nare not cumulative requirements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/reproducibility/principles-guidelines-reporting-preclinical-research\">NIH\n– Principles and Guidelines for Reporting Preclinical Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nReplicates, statistical reporting, randomization and blinding; indexed\npage · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41592-023-01987-9\">Community-developed\nchecklists for publishing images and image analyses (2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nReporting acquisition, processing and analysis; indexed article · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.15252/msb.20188746\">Luecken &amp; Theis\n(2019) – Current best practices in single-cell RNA-seq analysis: a\ntutorial<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Quality control, normalization, annotation and inference\npitfalls; indexed article · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23219535/\">Albeck, Mills\n&amp; Brugge (2013) – Frequency-modulated pulses of ERK activity\ntransmit quantitative proliferation signals<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Abstract: temporal ERK\nactivity and cell-to-cell variability; indexed abstract · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
